Paper: Error Detection and Correction Codes for Safe In-Memory Computations


Context & Problem

In-Memory Computing (IMC) accelerators reduce AI inference latency and energy, but device-level variations in FeFET and RRAM technologies introduce soft faults that significantly degrade neural network accuracy. Existing approaches—like arithmetic/non-arithmetic error codes or Triple Modular Redundancy (TMR)—often require large area overheads (≈200%) and do not fully leverage NN fault tolerance. 

Core Contribution

The paper introduces Neural Checksums, a lightweight, accuracy-driven runtime error detection and correction mechanism for IMC. It uses:

Crossbar Checksum – redundant columns inside each Processing Element (PE).

PE Checksum – redundant PEs computing column-wise sums across PEs.

These two independent safety blocks enable real-time fault localization and error correction inside IMC without heavy redundancy.
